}\uc1\pard\ul\f0\fs24 1.00 Introduction\ulnone\par
\par
\f1 P?S/8 supports a general-purpose assembler for \ul\i PDP-8 Assembly Language\ulnone\i0  (\i PAL\i0 ) known as \i P?S/8\i0  \i\f2 PAL\i0\f0 .  This document describes all technical aspects of P?S/8 PAL in terms of both language features and constructive usage; this includes certain tips and techniques for\par
PAL programming helpful to all users regardless of assembly language skill level.\par
\par
Note: The current release of P?S/8 PAL is Version 8S which is provided as part of P?S/8 Version 8Z; this includes a small number of official patches made since the original 06-Oct-1987 release.  Specific patch details can be found within the \ul\i P?S/8 System Change Log\ulnone\i0  maintained in a separate document.\par
\par
LINC mode assembly (as used on LINC, LINC-8 and PDP-12 computers) is enabled by the use of certain command-line options; the implementation is generally compatible with the \i LAP6-DIAL\i0 /\i DIAL-MS\i0  embedded assembler utility.\par
\par
Note: For more information concerning dual-mode assembly, see Sections 2.10 and 2.11 below which document dual-mode assembly considerations.\par
While appropriate LINC mode hardware is necessary to run binary programs containing LINC mode instructions, P?S/8 PAL runs entirely in PDP-8 mode on any supported P?S/8 configuration.\par
\par
Unlike competing assemblers (\i TOPS-10\i0  \i PAL10\i0  and \i OS/8\i0  \i PAL8\i0 ), there are many command-line option switches supported by P?S/8 PAL; this is necessary to allow the flexible and modular sections of P?S/8 PAL to be invoked as needed.  Various sections of this document will explain these command-line switches including information on how best to deploy them for any given assembly scenario.  Not every feature of\par
P?S/8 PAL is needed in most typical situations; as such, it is recommended that only relevant option switches be set during any particular assembly operation.\line\line Note: \f1 Official releases of TOPS-10 PAL10 are of limited use for serious PDP-8 program development as compared to either P?S/8 PAL or OS/8 PAL8.  This is due to a small number of important features that are either lacking entirely or are misimplemented; as such, PAL10 is unusable for a significant amount of PDP-8 program development.\par
\par
This is ironic since at one point, most PDP-8 program development (outside of the \ul\i R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0  and all but the earliest generations of P?S/8) was generally performed on TOPS-10 systems to take advantage of the then-rich feature set of PAL10 and the advanced system features of TOPS-10 for program developers using any language.\par
\par
It is hoped some proficient PDP-10 developers can release an updated PAL10 generally compatible with at least the crucial features that distinguish P?S/8 PAL and PAL8 from the present PAL10 release.  The developers of P?S/8 will gladly discuss these issues (and of course recommend that P?S/8 PAL features beyond PAL8 compatibility also be included).\page An important feature of the PAL language is the availability of several forms of literal statements; sections 2.06 through 2.09 below cover various aspects of literal usage and recommended syntax.\par
\par
Fortunately, the current PAL10 literal implementation is largely compatible with PAL8 (which is in turn a subset of the P?S/8 PAL literal implementation).\par
\par
The primary difference between the P?S/8 PAL literal implementation and that of PAL10 (and PAL8) is the conditional (or dependent) literal, a feature unique to P?S/8 PAL.  Appendix C below discusses numerous issues related to conditional literals.\par
\par
\ul\f0 1.10 P?S/8 keyboard monitor commands to invoke P?S/8 PAL\ulnone\par
\par
Command-line options are passed within P?S/8 in the same manner in which OS/8 \ul\i Concise Command Language\ulnone\i0  (\i CCL\i0 ) commands are formed (with certain extensions).\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports several command prompt variations.  The prompt used throughout this document is identical to that used in TOPS-10 and OS/8 to facilitate comparisons between the PAL implementations in the various operating systems.  \cf1\highlight2 <SP>\cf0\highlight0  and \cf1\highlight2 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  are freely allowed between command elements to enhance command readability and are not otherwise required.  For a complete discussion of P?S/8 command prompt options, consult documentation of the P?S/8 \i SET\i0  command available in a separate document.\par
\par
Typical command examples (in slightly simplified form) are structured as follows:\par
\par
.PAL \cf1\highlight2 output files\cf0\highlight0  < \cf1\highlight2 input files\cf0\highlight0  \cf1\highlight2 option switches\cf0\highlight0\par
\i\par
or\i0\par
\par
.PAL \cf1\highlight2 input files\cf0\highlight0  > \cf1\highlight2 output files\cf0\highlight0\i  \cf1\highlight2\i0 option switches\cf0\highlight0\f2\par
\par
\f0 Note: The P?S/8 keyboard monitor allows command formation in either file specification order as shown above; this allows the user to work in a familiar environment regardless of prior experience (with various other DEC operating systems or similar).  The relevant commands are generally similar to various \ul\i concise command\ulnone\i0  implementations found in other DEC operating systems.\par
\par
The option switches can be placed anywhere in the command line after the program name (in this instance \i\f2 PAL\i0\f0 ) and may be stated redundantly.\par
\par
For more information about P?S/8 command formation and command-line variations, consult the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.\page\ul 1.11 PAL input files\ulnone\par
\par
The program input text to PAL is generally the concatenated contents of a group of \ul\i Tiny File System\ulnone\i0  (\i TFS\i0 ) files passed in the proper order within the input portion of the command.  Each TFS file is exactly 2048 12-bit words long; in general, each file will tend to contain up to 3/4 of this capacity as six-bit bytes in the P?S/8 ASCII text subset character set.  This corresponds to about 3000 characters per TFS file depending on average line width and attendant line number requirements.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 TFS files include line numbers which are ignored by P?S/8 PAL.  Outside of the edit buffer of the keyboard monitor (which can change the line numbers) these line numbers are permanent.  Most P?S/8 system programs ignore line number content within input files with the exception of several utilities specifically designed to display or otherwise interact with line numbers (if present).\par
\par
The maximum number of files that can be passed to any P?S/8 system program is 17.  Since several file slots may be needed for output files, the effective maximum number of input files tends to be somewhat smaller (perhaps around 14 input files total).\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 also supports extended-length input files, each of which can contain considerably more text characters than the entirety of 17 TFS files.  For more information regarding extended-length file considerations, consult documentation of the P?S/8 \i OS8CON\i0  program (or other similar file conversion utility programs) available as separate documents.\par
\par
\ul 1.12 PAL output files\ulnone\par
\par
As stated above, all P?S/8 TFS files are fixed size (2048 12-bit words).  TFS binary files are created in the versatile \i Slurp\i0  binary format, which is significantly more efficient than binary files based on paper-tape frame images (such as the standard DEC \i BIN\i0  loader format).  A typical TFS binary file contains nearly 6/7 as much binary data as a block-format core-image file of identical size without implementing any form of internal block structure.  Binary loaders for TFS binary files are available on all standard P?S/8 configurations; a generic virtual Slurp loader is implemented in all systems.  Many systems also support a device-specific Slurp loader (which will be the default loader if implemented).  Command-line option switches control which loader will be used in specific situations initiated by P?S/8 PAL command variations (\ul\i assemble then load and go\ulnone\i0  \i or\i0  \ul\i assemble then create bitmap then load and go\ulnone\i0 ).\par
\par
At the end of the assembly, the count of created binary files is displayed (along with other particulars); this allows any excess passed output files to be repurposed.\par
\par
Note: If insufficient output files are passed, a \cf1\highlight2 BO\cf0\highlight0  (Binary Overflow) error message will be displayed on the system console; the assembly process will be aborted.\page\ul 1.13 Command-line option switches\ulnone\par
\par
The command-line option switches are passed in a manner consistent with \ul\i Concise Command Language\ulnone\i0  (\i CCL\i0 ) as implemented in TOPS-10 and OS/8.  Two variant forms are supported as follows:\par
\par
1) One or more individual option switches each preceded by \cf1\highlight2 /\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab /M /N /L\par
\par
2) Grouped switches preceded by \cf1\highlight2 (\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab (MNL)\par
\par
Note: The trailing \cf1\highlight2 )\cf0\highlight0  is optional unless needed to delimit and separate the option group from another command-line section.\par
\par
Both forms of option switches can be used together in the same command; all option switches may be given redundantly without error.\par
\par
\ul 1.14 Passed numerical value\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL can optionally chain to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 , the binary file loading and utility program, for the purpose of loading (or paper-tape punching) the binary files created during the assembly.  If desired, the assembled program can be automatically started at an address expressed as =\i xxxx\i0  where \i xxxx\i0  is up to four octal digits in the range of 0000 through 7776; for programs starting in extended memory, the /1 through /7 switches provide the starting field.  See Sections 2.02 and 2.30 below for more information.\par
\par
The P?S/8 keyboard monitor internally passes the value 7777 when an explicit numerical value is not provided.  When binary file loading is in effect, BIN uses this value to cause program start at a system-specific location somewhere within the system kernel area (07600-07777); a safe halt instruction is loaded at the selected location.  Pressing \cf1\highlight2 Continue\cf0\highlight0  on the front panel will effectively loop in place preventing potential problems; the user is expected to start the program manually.\par
\par
Note: By use of the P?S/8 \i GET\i0  command (or adding the equivalent options to the P?S/8 PAL command line), a core-image of the program can be created at the end of the assembly process to allow further program development.  Debugging and related binary program options are described in a separate document.\par
\par
\ul 2.00 Command-line option descriptions\ulnone\par
\par
The following sections of this document describe command-line options grouped by common function.  External references to sorted command-line option lists and additional information are provided elsewhere in this document.  Appendix D below contains a list of all command line options in sorted order.\page\ul 2.01 Output file options\ulnone\par
\par
If explicit binary output files are not given in the command line, the following option switches can be used:\par
\par
\tab /B\tab The \ul\i %\ulnone\i0  file on the P?S/8 system device bootup logical unit \tab\tab\tab (see description of the /U option switch below) is used as \tab\tab\tab the (first) binary output file.\par
 \par
\tab /D\tab If the /B option switch is set, the \ul\i $\ulnone\i0  file on the P?S/8\par
\tab\tab system device bootup logical unit (see description of the /U \tab\tab option switch below) is used as the second binary output \tab\tab\tab file (if required).\par
\par
\tab /U\tab Change the logical unit that applies (when the /B and /D \tab\tab\tab option switches are used as described above) from the system \tab\tab device bootup logical unit to the result of the following \tab\tab\tab calculation: \{System device bootup logical unit\} \cf1\highlight2 XOR\cf0\highlight0  1\}.  \tab\tab\tab This selects the \i opposite\i0  logical unit of the primary pair\par
 \tab\tab of logical drive units.\par
\par
\tab Note: Certain P?S/8 systems can be booted to non-zero logical\par
\tab units; as such, this implementation method will generally allow a\par
\tab useful drive pairing.\par
\par
\tab Due to their physical proximity to the beginning of the storage \tab device, specifying the \ul %\ulnone  and \ul $\ulnone  files can greatly speed up various \tab system operations.  Additionally, these files are not subject to \tab the overhead of directory operations with regard to the TFS\par
\tab directory on the same logical device unit; as such, many \tab operations have significantly lower overhead when these files are \tab used strategically.  On tape-based systems, \tab throughput can be \tab further improved by using a logical unit different from the \tab system bootup logical unit.\par
\par
\tab Note: The /B, /D and /U option switches are provided to simplify \tab the process of allocating output files as most assemblies require \tab one or two output files.  All file specifications can designate \tab an optional device unit; as such, any combination of files can \tab explicitly state associated logical device units as necessary.  \tab The use of these option switches merely allows specifying\par
 \tab commonly used output file combinations in a more succinct form.\par
\par
\tab If more than two binary output files are required, or the created \tab binary files should be written to permanently named TFS files,\par
 \tab the command line must include explicitly stated output files.\par
\par
\tab Note: If the second binary output file is not required, the /D \tab option switch will be ignored (effectively reducing the command-\par
\tab line to have been given with only the /B option switch); the\par
\tab report given at the end of the assembly indicates the total count\par
\tab of binary output files actually required.\page\ul 2.02 Chaining options\par
\par
\ulnone\tab Note: Only binary output files created during the assembly\par
\tab process are used during chaining operations; excess output files\par
\tab (if any) are deleted from the passed file list.\ul\par
\par
\ulnone\tab Automatic chaining options can be invoked to further process \tab binary output files created during the assembly as follows:\par
\par
\tab\f2 /G\f0\tab\f2 The output files created during the assembly are passed as \f0\tab\tab\tab\f2 input files to the\f0  \f2 P?S/8 BIN utility for loading\f0  and \tab\tab\tab\tab execution\f2  \f0 (\f2 or\f0  punching binary paper-tapes as described \tab\tab\tab below in Section 2.30); a\f2 dditional\f0  \f2 loading \f0 (or punching)\par
\tab\tab\f2 option\f0 s\f2  may\f0  be applied as necessary.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The chain to BIN is prevented if there were any\par
\tab\tab assembly errors to avoid processing erroneous data.\par
\par
\tab\tab With regard to chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of\par
\tab\tab loading binary files, see Section 2.30 below for more\par
\tab\tab information; additional binary chaining options include \tab\tab\tab the /H, /I, /V and /Z command-line option switches and the\par
\tab\tab\f2 =\i\f0 xxxx\i0  passed numerical value (possibly in conjunction with\par
\tab\tab the /1 through /7 option switches).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The /W option switch must be clear to allow binary \tab\tab\tab file loading.\par
\par
\tab\tab With regard to chaining to P?S/8 BIN for the purpose of\par
\tab\tab punching binary paper-tape output, see Section 2.30 below \tab\tab\tab for more information; additional binary chaining options \tab\tab\tab include the /F and /R command-line option switches.\par
\par
  \tab\tab Note: The /W option switch must be set when punching binary \tab\tab paper-tape output.\par
\par
\tab /M\tab The output files created during the assembly are passed as \tab\tab\tab input files to the P?S/8 \i MAP\i0  utility to create a bitmap of\par
 \tab\tab the assembled program indicating actual memory usage.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The format of P?S/8 MAP bitmap output is generally \tab\tab\tab consistent with the output produced by PAL10 after any FIELD\par
\tab\tab directive is used (or at the end of assembly) and is a\par
\tab\tab superset of the output of OS/8 \i BITMAP\i0 .  The title format and\par
\tab\tab pagination specifics are the same as those used for the\par
\tab\tab assembler listing output (if any) and assembly statistics\par
\tab\tab pages (which in part depend on the usage of the /N option\par
\tab\tab switch as described in Section 2.05 below).\par
\par
\tab\tab It is possible to specify chaining options to P?S/8 MAP and\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 BIN in a single command; the chain to P?S/8 MAP will \tab\tab\tab always be performed first.  The further chain to P?S/8 BIN\par
\tab\tab will be prevented if assembly errors occurred.\page\tab\tab Note: When the P?S/8 MAP utility is used without chaining, \tab\tab\tab certain option switches can be used to limit which memory\par
\tab\tab fields are eligible for mapping; during the chaining process\par
\tab\tab this feature is inhibited.  As such, all memory fields are\par
\tab\tab eligible for bitmap output following the assembly; the\par
\tab\tab output is limited to actually processed fields.\par
\par
\ul 2.03 Pass control options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /K\tab Perform a two-pass assembly; the default is a single \tab\tab\tab\tab assembly pass.  Note: The /K option switch is not required \tab\tab\tab if any binary or listing options are specified, as these \tab\tab\tab functions intrinsically require two pass operation.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unlike PAL8 which always performs two (or three) passes,\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL can perform a useful single-pass assembly (which\par
\tab\tab is consistent with the paper-tape system assembly program\par
\tab\tab known as \ul\i PAL III\ulnone\i0 ).\par
\par
\tab\tab At the end of pass one, all symbols still not defined are \tab\tab\tab shown on the system console in sorted order; the value \tab\tab\tab indicated is the address within the assembly where each \tab\tab\tab undefined symbol was first encountered.  This facilitates \tab\tab\tab early program development when it is likely that portions \tab\tab\tab of the program have not yet been completed.  Alternatively,\par
\tab\tab variables and temporaries may not yet be defined (or perhaps \tab\tab are the object of typographical errors which can cause \tab\tab\tab problems when certain symbols have names similar to that of \tab\tab other symbols); any problem of this nature can generally be\par
 \tab\tab quickly resolved by merely referencing the relatively short \tab\tab list of undefined symbols.\par
\par
\tab\tab Contrast this functionality with that provided by PAL10 or\par
\tab\tab PAL8, neither of which support a mechanism to highlight\par
\tab\tab undefined symbols.  The only facility provided in these\par
\tab\tab assembly programs is to create a second pass (or third\par
 \tab\tab pass) listing file of the entire assembly; searching through \tab\tab this far more voluminous output can be tedious as there are \tab\tab usually several references to each undefined symbol as well \tab\tab as many lines of output unrelated to the error particulars.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: In perverse instances, large quantities of erroneous\par
\tab\tab output could be triggered by the failure to correctly define \tab\tab key symbols during early program development; these \tab\tab\tab\tab circumstances could make the manual process of eliminating \tab\tab\tab errors far worse due to cascading dependencies.\page\tab\tab As such, using PAL10 or PAL8 can easily become a time-\par
\tab\tab consuming process.  In such circumstances, it would be\par
\tab\tab prudent to limit the assembly to a single pass using\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL until key problems are solved.  Using only the\par
\tab\tab undefined symbol report (available at the end of pass one of\par
\tab\tab the P?S/8 PAL assembly), most problematic issues can\par
\tab\tab generally be eliminated.  Using a second pass listing\par
\tab\tab provided by P?S/8 PAL would be as frustrating to use as that\par
\tab\tab obtained from PAL8; however, only P?S/8 PAL provides the one\par
\tab\tab pass method that is recommended for early program\par
\tab\tab development.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: See the description of the /L and /N option switches\par
 \tab\tab for the alternate usage of the /K option switch.\par
\par
\f2 Second \f0 pass \f2 assembly considerations.\par
\par
\f0\tab A second assembly pass is performed if binary output files are \tab created and/or assembly listing output is produced.  During pass \tab two, any additional error messages will be displayed on the \tab prevailing output device (system console or lineprinter) in a \tab format similar to that of PAL10 and PAL8 (including the nearest\par
\tab symbol plus any numerical offset) to facilitate source code\par
\tab development; in certain instances additional information may be\par
\tab displayed.\par
\par
\tab Programmers may embellish the displayed output of the assembly \tab process when critical sections are being processed:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab P?S/8 PAL supports the ERROR directive; this causes a \tab\tab\tab\tab deliberate assembly error.  By using the ERROR directive, \tab\tab\tab the programmer can design internal safeguards to prevent \tab\tab\tab logical inconsistencies that might occur during program \tab\tab\tab maintenance.\par
\par
\tab\tab The design of safeguarded code sections generally includes\par
\tab\tab the use of conditional assembly statements to calculate\par
\tab\tab problematic scenarios; as long as certain specific \par
\tab\tab requirements are met, there will be no ERROR directive \tab\tab\tab output.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The output of the ERROR directive is consistent with\tab\tab\tab that of all standard errors during each assembly pass and \tab\tab\tab can include the display of an optional evaluated argument. \tab\tab\tab Automatic binary loading by chaining to P?S/8 BIN will be \tab\tab\tab aborted if the ERROR directive was processed.\par
\par
\tab b)\tab P?S/8 PAL supports the PAUSE directive both as originally\par
\tab\tab implemented in PAL III (to ignore the rest of the contents\par
\tab\tab of the latest input file) and also as a language extension\par
\tab\tab feature which can embellish displayed output during the\par
\tab\tab assembly process.\page\tab\tab When the PAUSE directive is used with an argument, the \tab\tab\tab argument is evaluated as a 12-bit unsigned value.  Displayed \tab\tab output is consistent with the format of standard error \tab\tab\tab messages; however, PAUSE usage does not report as an error.\par
\par
\tab\tab The programmer can use the PAUSE directive for a variety of \tab\tab purposes (such as announcing the assembly pass or start of \tab\tab\tab assembly of a critical section).  When a logical \tab\tab\tab\tab\tab inconsistency is detected, the PAUSE directive can be used \tab\tab\tab to output additional information about the error to help \tab\tab\tab maintenance programmers understand the nature of the\tab\tab\tab\tab problem.\par
\par
\tab\tab The techniques described above and other features unique to \tab\tab P?S/8 PAL can be qualified to prevent use with incompatible \tab\tab assemblers.\par
\par
\tab\tab The PQS directive is defined only in P?S/8 PAL; this allows \tab\tab the programmer to create conditional assembly statements to \tab\tab restrict usage of these features to P?S/8 PAL and not \tab\tab\tab\tab generate erroneous errors caused by the failure to support \tab\tab\tab the features in other assemblers.  An example of this usage\par
\tab\tab is as follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab IFNDEF\tab PQS\tab <PQS=  0>\tab /ZERO ONLY IF NOT P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab IFNZRO\tab PQS\tab <PAUSE .>\tab /DISPLAY CURRENT ORIGIN.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The XLIST directive, as implemented in P?S/8 PAL, is \tab\tab\tab consistent with PAL10 and PAL8; XLIST statements can be used\par
\tab\tab to prevent output of portions of an assembly as necessary\par
\tab\tab such as assembler-specific statements.  Deployed\par
\tab\tab judiciously, all unassembled conditional assembly can be\par
\tab\tab hidden from view when producing listing output.\par
\par
\ul 2.04 Symbol table options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /S\tab The symbol table will be output at the end of the assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the /N option switch is also set, the symbol table format \tab\tab will be consistent with that of an enhanced listing pass (if \tab\tab enabled).\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The exact output may be subject to the number of \tab\tab\tab assembly passes performed; two pass assembly is performed if \tab\tab binary output files are created and/or listing output is \tab\tab\tab produced, or the /K option switch is set.\par
\par
\tab /A\tab If the /S option switch is set, the entire symbol table will \tab\tab be output at the end of the assembly; this includes all \tab\tab\tab permanent symbols and assembly directives.\page\tab\tab Note: If dual-mode (PDP-8 and LINC) assembly is in effect, \tab\tab\tab the symbol table will be set to that which applies at the \tab\tab\tab end of the assembly; certain symbols with dual definitions \tab\tab\tab will display values as used during the final assembly mode.\par
\par
\tab\tab Certain permanent symbols may have been redefined by the \tab\tab\tab source code; this is especially important in LINC mode \tab\tab\tab assembly where there may be multiple definitions of certain \tab\tab instructions that are model-dependent within the various \tab\tab\tab LINC implementations.\par
\par
\tab\tab While all default \i LMODE\i0  definitions within P?S/8 PAL are for \tab\tab the PDP-12 LINC variant, source code changes can be made to\tab\tab\tab redefine the affected symbols for compatibility with the \tab\tab\tab LINC-8 or classic LINC as required.  Permanent change of\par
\tab\tab certain \i SKP\i0  class or other LINC instructions can only be\par
\tab\tab made at the source-code level of P?S/8 PAL; as such, it is\par
\tab\tab recommended that all affected symbols be redefined in user\par
 \tab\tab source code statements as required.\par
\par
\ul 2.05 Listing options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /L\tab Assembly listing output will be produced.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unlike PAL10 and PAL8, P?S/8 PAL performs at most two\par
\tab\tab passes.  P?S/8 PAL is capable of creating binary file and\par
\tab\tab listing output simultaneously during pass two; as such, the\par
\tab\tab following situation can be avoided:\par
\par
\tab\tab Obscure PAL language quirks can be problematic when literals\par
\tab\tab are in effect.  Novice programmers must avoid certain\par
\tab\tab scenarios where the definition of certain symbols and values\par
\tab\tab is dependent on the number of passes performed as well as\par
\tab\tab specific addresses of generated literals.  While this is\par
\tab\tab very bad programming technique (and avoided by experienced\par
\tab\tab programmers), it can be confusing to debug mystifying \par
\tab\tab results obtained from PAL8; PAL8 binary output is created\par
\tab\tab using symbolic definitions obtained at the end of pass one\par
\tab\tab possibly updated during pass two.  However, PAL8 listing\par
\tab\tab output is created using symbolic definitions obtained at the\par
\tab\tab end of pass two and possibly updated during pass three.\tab\tab\tab Unless the programmer is careful to avoid pass-dependent\par
\tab\tab definitions, the binary output may differ from the values\par
\tab\tab indicated in the listing output (which may appear correct).\par
\tab\tab Since P?S/8 PAL always creates all output during pass two,\par
\tab\tab there cannot be pass-dependent differences between binary\par
\tab\tab output and listing output.  Relevant programming blunders\par
\tab\tab will be discovered sooner using P?S/8 PAL (along with\par
\tab\tab additional experience when the improper techniques are\par
\tab\tab recognized and avoided).\page\tab /P\tab Enable wide-printing listing output.  Note: This option \tab\tab\tab also affects symbol table output allowing additional columns \tab\tab of printed symbols per page.  (The expected output format \tab\tab\tab changes from 72/80 columns to 132 columns as implemented on\par
 \tab\tab lineprinters that use 11" x 17" ledger/tabloid paper stock.)\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL8 only outputs to a minimal line width to guarantee\par
\tab\tab compatibility with narrow-carriage devices such as the LP01\par
\tab\tab lineprinter, despite the fact that most printers support 132\par
\tab\tab column mode; outputting to an LP01 may cause excessive\par
\tab\tab truncation.\par
\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL supports source statements significantly wider \par
\tab\tab than PAL8.  Where relevant, the listing output of P?S/8\par
\tab\tab PAL will avoid truncation unlike PAL8 when assembling the\par
\tab\tab identical source code.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL8 users must exercise caution when creating\par
\tab\tab (somewhat) wide program statements.  Due to quirks in the\par
\tab\tab PAL8 implementation, the precise number of characters\par
 \tab\tab leading to truncation problems varies with statement type\par
 \tab\tab and other unexpected issues; depending on specific\par
\tab\tab circumstances, a portion of the intended source statement\par
\tab\tab may be truncated; this can unexpectedly change logical\par
\tab\tab program flow.\par
\par
\tab\tab Warning: This is not merely a matter of truncating the\par
\tab\tab listing output; rather, part of the source code statement\par
\tab\tab may be ignored.  Debugging unexpectedly truncated assembly\par
\tab\tab statements may be difficult (especially when conditional\par
\tab\tab assembly techniques are used in conjunction with the XLIST\par
\tab\tab directive).\par
\par
\tab /T\tab Prevent output to the system lineprinter.  All output is \tab\tab\tab directed to the system console terminal.  See the \tab\tab\tab\tab description of the /N option switch below.\par
\par
\tab /N\tab Output \i niceties\i0  are applied to the listing output including\par
\tab\tab titles on each printed page.  If output is directed to the\par
\tab\tab system console (instead of the system lineprinter), the\par
\tab\tab output will produce simulated form-feeds by the use of tear-\tab\tab off lines instead of \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  characters; this will ensure\par
 \tab\tab compatibility with system console devices that use roll\par
\tab      paper such as the Teletype Model 33.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: For systems lacking a lineprinter that support hard \tab\tab\tab copy console terminals such as the LA36 (which can properly \tab\tab support \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  characters), the \ul\i Logical Console Overlay\ulnone\i0  can be \tab\tab configured to redirect lineprinter output to the same\par
 \tab\tab physical device as that used for console output.\par
\par
\tab\tab When the /N option switch is enabled, one additional page of \tab\tab memory is allocated.  See Section 2.13 below regarding\par
 \tab\tab memory management options for more information.\page\tab /K\tab Enable assembly title text from each input file.\par
\par
\tab\tab When the /L option switch is used in conjunction with the /N \tab\tab option switch, the title field text for the entire assembly\par
\tab\tab is taken from the first line of the first file.\par
\par
\tab\tab Typically, assemblies are processed by concatenating the\par
\tab\tab contents of multiple TFS text files.  In most cases there is\par
\tab\tab no particular connection between program sections in\par
\tab\tab different files and intended assembly title changes; file\par
\tab\tab boundaries are merely a file contents consideration.\par
\par
\tab\tab To maintain compatibility with PAL10 and PAL8, taking the\par
\tab\tab title field characters from the first line of every input\par
\tab\tab file can be enabled; the /K option switch must be given\par
\tab\tab along with the /L and /N option switches.  As long as each\par
\tab\tab logically independent section of a program is contained\par
\tab\tab within either an extended-length file (or a single TFS file\par
\tab\tab such as a small parameter setup file), meaningful title\par
\tab\tab changes can be obtained.\par
\par
\tab\tab As a practical matter, intentional use of assembly title\par
\tab\tab field updates via file breaks is used infrequently.  For \tab\tab\tab example, the source code of \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone\i0  updates the title \tab\tab\tab field only once during the entire assembly; as such, two\par
\tab\tab extended-length files (for the main modules known\par
\tab\tab respectively as \i FOCAL\i0  and \i FLOAT\i0 ) support the intended title\par
\tab\tab field changes during the assembly.\par
\tab\tab\par
\tab\tab Additionally, the title on the assembly can also be changed\par
\tab\tab using other methods that are independent of file boundaries.\par
\tab\tab See the description of the \i EJECT\i0  and \i TITLE\i0  directives in\par
\tab\tab Appendix B below for further information.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: A two-pass assembly will occur because the /L option\par
\tab\tab switch is set to create listing output.  As such, the /K\par
\tab\tab option switch should only be used to enable title field text\par
\tab\tab from every input file.  The alternate usage of the /K switch\par
\tab\tab (to force a two-pass assembly) is moot.\par
\par
\tab /X\tab Enable the creation of cross-reference output by loading the \par
\tab\tab cross-reference module; this will output all symbolic and \tab\tab\tab literal references (if any) as well as the statement number \tab\tab associated with the reference.  Cross-reference data is \tab\tab\tab created in statement order during the assembly; at the end \tab\tab\tab of the assembly the data is sorted and included as part of \tab\tab\tab the overall listing output.\par
\par
\tab\tab Enabling cross-reference output causes all listing output to \tab\tab be somewhat wider because all printed lines start with the \tab\tab\tab lowest statement number on the line.  (The PAL language \tab\tab\tab allows multiple statements on a line separated by \highlight3 ;\highlight0 .)  \tab\tab\tab Statement numbers must be in the range of 1 through 99999.\page\tab\tab Note: It is strongly recommended that listing output with \tab\tab\tab statement numbering be printed on a 132 column lineprinter, \tab\tab especially if the /P option is also enabled.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the /A option switch is also enabled, all symbolic \tab\tab\tab\tab references will generate cross-reference output; the default \tab\tab is references to user symbols only.\par
\par
\tab\tab When the /X option switch is enabled, two additional pages \tab\tab\tab of memory are allocated.  See Section 2.13 of this document\par
 \tab\tab regarding memory management options for more information.\par
\par
\tab\tab Implementation Restrictions.\par
\par
\tab\tab Due to planned changes in P?S/8 Version 9A (but not yet \tab\tab\tab implemented), the cross-reference output is currently \tab\tab\tab\tab written to the system device logical unit 7 starting at \tab\tab\tab block 0000; all prior contents of this device will be \tab\tab\tab\tab overwritten.  No additional output of cross-reference data \tab\tab\tab is available; further, no final processing is provided in \tab\tab\tab the current release.\par
\par
\tab\tab Once the cross-reference output programming is upgraded to\par
\tab\tab allow allocation of extended-length files via a user-\tab\tab\tab\tab settable configuration table, this restriction will be \tab\tab\tab lifted; significantly larger programs will be handled than \tab\tab\tab presently on appropriate configurations (such as RK8E/RK05-\tab\tab\tab based systems) due to the availability of multiple extended-\tab\tab length files to store reference data during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab As of this writing, the processing of the cross-reference \tab\tab\tab output is not fully implemented pending the changes \tab\tab\tab\tab described above with regard to the cross-reference output \tab\tab\tab data; however both statement numbering and creation of \tab\tab\tab reference data are functional.  Logical unit 7 must be \tab\tab\tab available as described above.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL8 does not directly support cross-reference output.\par
\tab\tab As an alternative, OS/8 listing files are reparsed by OS/8\par
\tab\tab CREF (if enabled) as a post-processing utility.  There are\par
\tab\tab several disadvantages to this method:\par
\par
\tab a)\tab The listing output of PAL8 is not a statement-by-statement \tab\tab\tab listing file; statements hidden by the use of the XLIST\par
\tab\tab directive (and related techniques) do not appear in the\par
\tab\tab listing file.  As such, the cross-referenced listing output\par
\tab\tab is potentially incomplete; statement numbers are inherently\par
\tab\tab inaccurate and untrustworthy.\par
\par
\tab\tab By contrast, P?S/8 PAL statement numbering is compatible \tab\tab\tab with PAL10, which correctly shows statement numbers\par
 \tab\tab revealing any hidden statements.\page\tab b)\tab A valid technique for including documentation within PAL \tab\tab\tab source code is to use deliberately failing conditional \tab\tab\tab assembly on a section of commentary as large as an entire \tab\tab\tab printable page of the listing.  Content examples include\par
\tab\tab details regarding program operation and \ul\i banner\i0  \i pages\ulnone\i0  of\par
\tab\tab large character outlines created from a series of smaller\par
 \tab\tab characters; the nature of this form of output is limited \tab\tab\tab only by the programmer's creativity.\par
\par
\tab\tab Unfortunately, CREF has no ability to discern this aspect of\par
\tab\tab a PAL8 listing file as commentary; as such, the commentary\par
 \tab\tab is cross-referenced as if part of assembled code-generating\par
\tab\tab statements.  When banner pages are used, there can be\par
\tab\tab numerous erroneous junk references to \i symbols\i0  that are \tab\tab\tab merely the individual characters used to build up the larger\par
 \tab\tab character layouts; as such, the cross-reference output can \tab\tab\tab be cluttered with many erroneous references.  Since CREF has\par
\tab\tab limitations on overall symbol table size, this can have a\par
\tab\tab deleterious effect on the overall capability of CREF.  See\par
\tab\tab c) and d) below.\par
\par
\tab c)\tab CREF internally reckons statement numbers as 13-bit integers\par
\tab\tab in the range of 1 through 8191 with an odd quirk: additional\par
\tab\tab statements wrap back to 4096 instead of 1.  As such, there\par
\tab\tab is inherent anomaly in the cross-reference output regarding\par
\tab\tab precise statement numbering of individual references.\par
\par
\tab\tab By contrast, P?S/8 PAL and PAL10 allow unique statement\par
\tab\tab numbering in the range of 1 through 99999.\par
\par
\tab d)\tab CREF has limited overall capability.  If the entire cross-\par
\tab\tab reference cannot be accomplished in one pass the program\par
\tab\tab aborts unless the /M option switch is used.  If the\par
 \tab\tab /M option switch is enabled, CREF operates as a strictly-\tab\tab\tab defined two-pass operation which may (eventually) allow the \tab\tab program to complete.  However, each \i half\i0  of the overall \tab\tab\tab operation must succeed to obtain a successful overall\tab  \tab\tab\tab result.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: A quirk of the two-pass method is the insertion of an \tab\tab extraneous \highlight3 <FF>\highlight0  in the output stream when the second pass \tab\tab\tab starts.  (This is documented but not explained.)\par
\par
 \tab\tab The split-point for this so-called \i mammoth\i0  mode is set to\par
 \tab\tab the theoretical symbol \i LGNNNN\i0 .  References to all symbols \tab\tab\tab that sort lower than this value are scanned in the first \tab\tab\tab pass while ignoring references to symbols that sort higher. \tab\tab The second pass processes all symbols that sort higher than \tab\tab\i LGNNNN\i0  including all references to current-page and page \tab\tab\tab zero literals; the references processed in the first pass \tab\tab\tab are ignored.  Each pass must scan the entire listing file.\page\tab\tab Because of the arbitrary nature of the split-point, it is \tab\tab\tab possible for the overall cross-reference to fail because the \tab\tab program symbols are not \i balanced\i0  sufficiently to conform \tab\tab\tab to this arbitrary scheme (for which there are no options).\par
\par
\tab e)\tab Because of the inherently redundant nature of rescanning \tab\tab\tab text files attempting to obtain information about the \tab\tab\tab\tab assembly without having access to the original symbol table \tab\tab and parsing techniques, CREF operates very slowly.\par
\par
\tab\tab By contrast, assembler-based cross-reference implementation \tab\tab has the advantage of only processing legitimate references \tab\tab\tab to symbols and literals; commentary is ignored.  Instead of \tab\tab rescanning entire listing files, the essential cross-\tab\tab\tab\tab reference data is scanned as many times as necessary to \tab\tab\tab process all references; there is no arbitrary static split-\tab\tab\tab point nor requirement of at most two passes on the far \tab\tab\tab smaller set of data.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Many large PAL programs cannot be completely processed \tab\tab by CREF.  This includes (ironically) the source code of\par
\tab\tab P?S/8 PAL.\par
\par
\ul 2.06 Literal and link options\par
\par
\ulnone Properly utilized, PDP-8 literal statements allow rapid implementation of working programs.  That said, certain compromises may arise:\line\line Often a PDP-8 programmer is concerned with producing the smallest possible coding for a given implementation.  Certain portions of operating systems have stringent coding requirements with regard to an extremely limited program space.  Often the expediency of using literals must be abandoned in favor of numerous tricks for which there is no analogue in other computer architectures.\line\line a) Intrepid programmers can created needed literal values in various\par
   ways.  The techniques generally use either the contrived location\par
   of instructions to force generation of needed constants or the use\par
   of seemingly superfluous OPR statement conditions that can create\par
   fortuitous constants regardless of code position.  In rare cases,\par
   multiple contrivances are used in consort to achieve even tighter\par
   binary code.  The ability to perform extreme optimization\par
   techniques requires intimate knowledge of the PDP-8 instruction set\par
   in terms of operation code values, etc.\line\line b) When the contrived literal values are achieved, the use of\par
   generated literal statements must be abandoned where applicable.\par
   Thus, this extreme form of optimization is contrary to the\par
   convenience of using generated literals.\page c) Purely for documentation purposes, some programmers use source code\par
   statements that include the syntax of the equivalent literal.\par
   However, that portion of the statement is part of an extended\par
   comment.  This essentially explains the intentions of the\par
   programmer prior to the optimization.\line\line d) In general, the use of literals discourages code space\par
   optimization; as such, literals should be used if as and when\par
   appropriate.  Many operating system components contain once-only\par
   initialization sections which are later repurposed as buffers or\par
   table/list space.  It is totally appropriate to include literal\par
   statements within such code; generally, there is little to be\par
   gained using optimization techniques.\par
\par
e) Beginning programmers are encouraged to learn the proper use of\par
   literals just after mastering the general instruction set; code\par
   optimization is never a factor until the programmer is quite\par
   experienced.  As required, programs can be (partially) optimized by\par
   removing literal statements as appropriate once the complexity of\par
   the project demands optimization after the basic structure has been\par
   achieved.\par
\par
\f1 Since PDP-8 code is generally efficient for most applications, a common technique is the use of literals during early development, perhaps in conjunction with conditional literals as described below.  Only when necessary, optimization techniques can be applied (while simultaneously removing literal statements).\par
\par
Many of the system components of P?S/8 do not use literals while other sections use literals in at least one of several contexts usually related to program initialization.  This includes the implementation of P?S/8 PAL itself.  Most conforming user-written programs use identical techniques during development.\par
\par
In P?S/8 PAL literals and/or generated links are enabled using a combination of option switches as follows:\par
\par
\f0\tab /Q\tab Enable generation of current page and page zero literals.  \tab\tab\tab Link generation is disabled; attempts at off-page references \tab\tab will be treated as errors.\par
\par
\tab /O\tab Enable generation of current page and page zero literals.  \tab\tab\tab Link generation is enabled; however, links will be flagged \tab\tab\tab as errors.\par
\par
\tab /E\tab Reset the current page literal extent when leaving the \tab\tab\tab latest page.  (Use of the /E option switch requires some \tab\tab\tab combination of the /Q and /O option switches to be \tab\tab\tab\tab meaningful.)\par
\par
\tab\tab Enabling literal generation and/or link generation requires \tab\tab three additional pages of allocated memory.  See Section \tab\tab\tab 2.13 of this document for more information regarding memory\par
\tab\tab allocation and related issues.\page\tab\tab Using the /Q option switch in conjunction with the /O option\par
\tab  \tab switch will suppress error messages on generated links. \tab\tab\tab However, the count of links generated is always included in\par
\tab\tab the statistics page produced at the end of the assembly.\par
\par
\tab\tab Assembly listing lines containing generated links will be\par
\tab\tab flagged with \highlight3\f2 '\highlight0\f0 ; this feature cannot be disabled.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: PAL8 and PAL10 are compatible with P?S/8 PAL with\par
\tab\tab regard to all aspects of the disposition of generated link\par
\tab\tab statements.  However, all features regarding literals in\par
\tab\tab PAL8 cannot be disabled.  See below for detailed warnings\par
\tab\tab regarding generated links that apply to all PDP-8 \par
 \tab\tab assemblers.\par
\par
\ul 2.07 Limitations of generated links\ulnone\par
\par
In general, generated links should be completely avoided; the short version: \b Generated links do not work!\par
\par
\b0 While this simple caveat ought to be sufficient, a short study of the PDP-8 architecture reveals specific problems as follows:\par
\par
1) Link generation is an attempt to partially abstract the PDP-8\par
   architecture.  Unfortunately, this leads to sloppy programming\par
   techniques that (might) function while violating the hard-wired\par
   rules of the PDP-8 instruction set.\par
\par
   A generated link is an indirect memory reference used to modify a\par
   malformed attempt at a direct memory reference that cannot be\par
   achieved; this is accomplished by using an address literal created\par
   in a manner analogous to that used within literal statements.\par
\par
   The program specifies a direct memory reference that is impossible\par
   due to the violation of memory addressing rules; instead an\par
   indirect reference is automatically created by the assembler\par
   (assuming this feature is enabled).  Since this process has much\par
   in common with the creation of literals, all other aspects of\par
   literal generation applies.\par
\par
   The following code fragment illustrates an attempt to violate the\par
   rules of the PDP-8 instruction set; the flawed statement is flagged\par
   as an error because link generation was not enabled.\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   IR  0577  \par
   000200  1377          TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\page    The attempted usage cannot be achieved since the operand is outside\par
   of the allowed address range (0200-0377 in this instance); location\par
   0577 is part of the following page (0400-0577).  For an example\par
   such as this, locations on any page from 2 (0400-0577) through 37\par
   (7600-7777) are equally inaccessible.  The instruction is flagged\par
   with the \highlight3 IR\highlight0  error message (Illegal Reference).\par
\par
   The next example is the identical code fragment assembled with link\par
   generation enabled.  There are no errors indicated, but the\par
   statement is flagged with \highlight3 '\highlight0  indicating link generation.\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   000200  1777'         TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\par
          *0377 \par
   000377  0577\par
\par
   The generated code is an indirect instruction in the range of 1600-\par
   1777 as a consequence of the modification; a direct instruction\par
   would be in the range of 1200-1377.  The code also creates an\par
   address constant; the table of literal/link addresses is dumped at\par
   the end of the assembly\par
\par
2) If link generation is enabled, the same source code produces\par
   flagged output.  The instruction at 0200 is changed to \ul\i TAD I 377\ulnone\i0 .\par
   Additionally, location 0377 is generated containing 0577.  Ignoring\par
   for the moment the instruction is now an indirect reference to 0577\par
   using the pointer address 0377 as an intermediary, to a naive PDP-8\par
   programmer (unaware of the potential negative consequences), the\par
   intended logic is satisfied: the contents of location 0577 are\par
   added to the accumulator.\par
\par
Note: In these coding examples, symbolic references are deliberately not used to prevent clouding the issues.  Actual attempts at link generation are usually in symbolic form; had the code been located in an accessible location, no link would be required.  Links often come about when code overflows the intended page into the next page.  By using purely numerical statements, the results are completely clear.\par
\par
3) To the posited programmer, the program fragment \ul\i works\i0  \i per se\ulnone\i0 .\par
   Unfortunately, in real PDP-8 programming, considerations outside of\par
   this example tend to crop up; programs that \ul\i had worked\ulnone\i0  suddenly \ul\i no\par
\ulnone\i0    \ul\i longer work\ulnone\i0  and might also cause unavoidable assembly errors of a\par
   kind that merely enabling link generation cannot remedy.\par
\par
   Such a programmer might be tempted to use a location already\par
   inaccessible (due to being on another page from the instruction) as\par
   a pointer.  The assembler will properly mark this with the error\par
   message \highlight3 II\highlight0  (Illegal Indirect).  Since it takes a pointer to access\par
   indirectly the address of the pointer, it is not possible to use it\par
   indirectly.  Had the programmer obeyed the rules of the PDP-8\par
   instruction set, this would have come about without sudden\par
   surprise.\page    Before investigating the insidious nature of link generation\par
   failing to work when no error messages occur (just generated link\par
   flagging), it will be shown that certain forms of generated links\par
   actually work as intended, as long as the mechanism is not pushed\par
   to the point of impossibility.\par
\par
   In all cases, links involving \ul\i Direct JMP\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Direct JMS\ulnone\i0  instructions\par
   properly function in the sense described above.  (Statements\par
   attempting link generation of the form \ul\i JMS I\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i JMP I\ulnone\i0  cannot\par
   function as explained above.)  If this were the only issue, lazy\par
   programmers might have a valid excuse to enable link generation.\par
\par
   Unfortunately, all of the other forms of link generation will fail\par
   depending on the exact nature of the running program; the assembler\par
   cannot have knowledge of the programmer's intentions in the dynamic\par
   sense.\par
\par
   Repeating the generated link example from above:\par
\par
          *0200          *200            /TYPICAL START ON PAGE 1.\par
\par
   000200  1777'         TAD     577     /THIS REALLY CAN'T WORK.\par
          *0377 \par
   000377  0577\par
\par
   A possible use of the example program includes manual start from\par
   the computer front panel; this allows setting the data field (DF)\par
   to a non-zero value in the range of 1 through 7.\par
\par
   When started this way, the program does not accomplish the intended\par
   function; the contents of location 0577 in the data field are added\par
   to the accumulator.\par
\par
   In general, PDP-8 programs are written for the 32K address space.\par
   The data field is often used as an internal convention of the\par
   program logic.  When the program uses direct references (as the\par
   nominal statements attempt to do in the example), the data field\par
   plays no role in the logic of the program.  Programmers who never\par
   enable link generation are certain their programs are designed to\par
   work as expected (as long as no other statements are flagged with\par
   errors).\par
\par
   Note: Lack of generated links is no guarantee there are no logic\par
   errors in the program; however, at least this area of bad design is\par
   not present.\par
\par
4) As soon as extended memory factors creep into the logic of a\par
   program dependent on link generation, insidious failures start to\par
   occur.  The programmer who never learned the discipline required to\par
   avoid this programming pitfall is now faced with a nearly insoluble\par
   dilemma.  The only remedy to this situation is to \ul\i bite the bullet\par
\ulnone\i0    so to speak, eliminating all generated links as a first step\par
   towards debugging the program; by preventing the attendant error\par
   messages much of the problem will be eliminated.\page    Note: Cleaning up after link generation might involve a large\par
   expenditure of effort all at once; had links never been used, the\par
   work necessary to eliminate assembly errors might have been\par
   accomplished in small increments.\par
\par
In short, link generation correctly functions only if \ul\i Direct JMP\ulnone\i0  or \ul\i Direct JMS\ulnone\i0  instructions are involved.  Link generation fails to \i work\i0  if any form of \ul\i AND, TAD, ISZ or DCA\ulnone\i0  instructions are involved.  All forms of indirect usage attempts always fail.\par
\par
Positing a stubborn and lazy PDP-8 programmer (the author has met people who fall into this category; their careers as PDP-8 programmers who use generated links are always short), one of those who insist on using link generation despite all the warnings; their excuses are invariably within the list as follows:\par
\par
   a) Use only \i JMP\i0  and \i JMS\i0  links. (This is not to be believed.)\par
\par
   b) Never use the cases that cannot work and count how many links\par
      are generated in the assembler statistics page every time to\par
      ensure nothing important has changed.\par
\par
      There are inherent logical fallacies in a) and b) above.\par
\par
   c) One can only assume a) is true; it is necessary to check the\par
      entire program to ensure this is the case.  Confirming this\par
      is far more work than merely avoiding link generation.\par
\par
   d) The count of links generated may be the same as in a previous\par
      assembly.  However, this is still no guarantee all of the\par
      generated links avoid the impossible cases; if the code is moved\par
      around sufficiently, a \i JMP\i0  or \i JMS\i0  link could drop out and be\par
      replaced with an impossible case in terms of the assembler\par
      generated links tally.  It is still necessary to carefully check\par
      the entire program as in c) above; the unfortunate need for\par
      additional work still applies.\par
\par
   e) In general, people this stubborn are hypocritical.  They will\par
      tend to use the potentially impossible form of generated links\par
      claiming certain knowledge of the data field throughout the\par
      entire program.  This makes them vulnerable to the various\par
      insidious problems described earlier.\par
\par
   f) When cross-field calling conventions are established in\par
      meaningful programs, it is likely belatedly understood just how\par
      problematic the burden of link generation actually is.  The work\par
      involved in elimination of the problems can be profound.\par
\par
   g) In the case of P?S/8 PAL, PAL10 and PAL8, additional option\par
      switches must be set to suppress error messages associated with\par
      link generation.  This is by design, as the developers of\par
      successful PDP-8 operating systems virtually never use link\par
      generation; they are trying to guide programmers to success in\par
      PDP-8 programming.\page\ul 2.08 Literal statement examples\ulnone\f2\par
\par
\f0 P?S/8 PAL supports the two general forms of literal generation along with a unique third form that can shorten program development time.\par
\par
\tab a)\tab Current page literals - The operand address is located on \tab\tab\tab the current page of the program; the address is assigned by \tab\tab the assembler starting at relative location 177.  Additional \tab\tab literals are created in reverse order; a second current page \tab\tab literal is placed at relative location 176.\par
\par
\tab\tab The maximum number of current page literals allowed is 63\tab\tab\tab (decimal) in the relative range of 101-177.  When a \tab\tab\tab\tab statement contains a current page literal, the current page \tab\tab literal table is searched for a matching value.  If \tab\tab\tab\tab successful, the existing literal address is used.  New \tab\tab\tab values create additional current page literals.  When either \tab\tab too many current page literals are attempted or statements \tab\tab\tab on the current page overflow into the literal table, a \highlight3 PE\highlight0\par
 \tab\tab (Page Exceeded) error message is displayed.\par
\par
\tab\tab A typical current page literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab TAD\tab (3)\tab\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The trailing right parenthesis character is optional\par
\tab\tab unless nested expressions are used.\par
\par
\tab b)\tab Page Zero literals - The operand address is located on Page \tab\tab Zero; the address is assigned by the assembler starting at \tab\tab\tab location 0177.  Additional literals are created in reverse \tab\tab\tab order; a second Page Zero literal is placed at location \tab\tab\tab 0176.\par
\par
\tab\tab The maximum number of Page Zero literals is 112 (decimal) in \tab\tab the range of 0020-0177.  (Locations lower than 0020 are \tab\tab\tab disallowed due to the special auto-index characteristics of\par
\tab\tab locations 0010-0017 when indirectly referenced.)\par
\par
\tab\tab When a statement contains a Page Zero literal, the Page Zero \tab\tab literal table is searched for a matching value.  If \tab\tab\tab\tab successful, the existing literal address is used.  New \tab\tab\tab values create additional Page Zero literals.  When either \tab\tab\tab too many Page Zero literals are attempted or statements on \tab\tab\tab Page Zero overflow into the literal table, a \highlight3 ZE\highlight0  Page Zero \tab\tab\tab Exceeded] error message is displayed.\par
\par
\tab\tab A typical page Zero literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab TAD\tab [3]\tab\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: The trailing right square bracket character is \tab\tab\tab\tab optional unless nested expressions are used.\page\tab c)\tab Conditional literals - The operand address is either on the \tab\tab current page or on Page Zero depending upon specific \tab\tab\tab\tab circumstances.  If a matching Page Zero literal value \tab\tab\tab\tab exists, it will be used.  Failing a Page Zero match, If a \tab\tab\tab matching current page literal exists, it will be used.  \tab\tab\tab Failing both a Page Zero match and a current page match, a \tab\tab\tab new current page literal is created (subject to the \tab\tab\tab\tab possibility of a \highlight3 PE\highlight0  error if the current page overflows).\par
\par
\tab\tab A typical conditional literal usage follows:\par
\par
\tab\tab TAD\tab #3 \tab\tab\tab\tab /ADD 0003 TO THE ACCUMULATOR.\par
\par
\tab\tab The actual code generated is dependent on the literal pools\par
\tab\tab in effect at the point in the assembly the usage is \tab\tab\tab\tab encountered.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: Since there is no corresponding closing character, it \tab\tab is impossible to create nested conditional literal\par
\tab\tab expressions.\par
\par
\tab\tab A common technique employed in well-developed programs is \tab\tab\tab the use of source code libraries of commonly used \tab\tab\tab\tab subroutines.  It is desirable to use Page Zero literals \tab\tab\tab where appropriate; however, Page Zero literals should not be \tab\tab used if there are only singular references.  By using  \tab\tab\tab conditional literals, more effective management of memory is \tab\tab achieved without the need to hand-optimize the library \tab\tab\tab source code statements containing literals.\par
\par
\tab\tab Additional information about conditional literals and \tab\tab\tab\tab related subjects can be found in Appendix C of this \tab\tab\tab\tab document.\par
\par
\tab\tab Note: This feature is not supported by PAL10 or PAL8.\par
\par
\ul 2.09 Literal extent issues\ulnone\par
\par
In general, all literals associated with any current page should be defined in proximate order to the code statements referencing them.  This facilitates locating any potential matches to avoid new literals (if possible).\par
\par
Leaving the present page, the existing literals are dumped to finish the intent and requirements of all source code statements defined so far.\par
\par
If that page is ever revisited during the assembly, it is possible to define additional literals on the page because the literal extent formerly in effect was saved and will be restored.  However, since the actual former literals have been dumped, all new literals will only be able to search for a match within the newly-created literal pool.\page Note: This feature, while enabled by default, is hardly ever used.  It is supported primarily to maintain compatibility with the literal generation features of PAL10 and PAL8.\par
\par
If the /E option switch is used, the literal extent when leaving a current page is totally reset; no history of previous literals on any current page is retained.\par
\par
Note: The use of the /E option switch does not impact page zero literals (which are completely reset upon either the end of the assembly or the use of the FIELD directive).  In certain PDP-8 documents, this is referred to as \i forgetting\i0  current page literals.\par
\par
Resetting literal extents is useful when defining overlays.  The overlay code must be created in the context of another section of the assembly which presumably will be replaced in memory by the overlay code; however, the overlay code must maintain its own current page literals as required.\par
\par
while there are many techniques associated with the deployment of overlays, the /E option switch is always required to allow overlays to maintain their own current page literal pools.\par
\par
Note: Future implementations of P?S/8 PAL (or a new assembler for the P?S/8 \i SHELL\i0  based on P?S/8 PAL tentatively to be known as \i XPAL\i0 ) will likely invert the default condition regarding literal extent retention due to disuse of the current default; as a practical matter, sections of program code that revisit formerly used pages are always part of an overlay structure.  There are no justifiable reasons to deliberately separate small program sections on the same code page.\par
\par
\ul 2.10 \f2 Dual \f0 a\f2 ssembly \f0 m\f2 ode \f0 o\f2 ptions\ulnone\par
\line\f0\tab /8\tab Enable LINC mode assembly; the default operating mode is \par
\tab\tab PDP-8 mode (\i PMODE\i0 ).  While not strictly necessary, it is \tab\tab\tab good practice when enabling dual assembly to state the\par
 \tab\tab desired opening mode; this is equivalent to using the PMODE\par
 \tab\tab directive.  The current location counter is set to the usual\tab\tab PDP-8 starting value of 0200.\par
\par
\tab /9\tab Enable LINC mode assembly; the default operating mode is \tab\tab\tab LINC mode (\i LMODE\i0 ).  While not strictly necessary, it is good \tab\tab practice when enabling dual assembly mode to state the \tab\tab\tab desired opening mode; this is equivalent to using the LMODE \tab\tab directive.  The current location counter is set to the usual \tab\tab LINC starting value of 4020.\par
\par
\tab\tab Enabling the dual mode assembly module requires the \tab\tab\tab\tab allocation of three additional pages of memory.  Consult \tab\tab\tab Section 2.13 of this document for more information regarding\par
 \tab\tab memory allocation and related issues.\page\ul 2.11 Dual mode assembly tips\ulnone\par
\par
While it is good practice to start source code with the appropriate mode directive, it is possible (using conditional assembly techniques) to determine the current assembly mode.  References to the current definition of the \i HLT\i0  instruction (which will be 7402 in PMODE and 0000 in LMODE) can determine whether LMODE or PMODE is in effect.\par
Critical programs can abort the assembly if it is determined the assembly process is not in keeping with source code requirements (which could include failure to enable LINC mode assembly).\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is the only known assembler that fully supports LINC mode assembly including conditionals and one's complement arithmetic, literals, large assemblies and the full complement of PDP-8 assembler features.  The LINC mode support is generally compatible with the \ul\i LAP6-DIAL\ulnone\i0 /\ul\i DIAL-MS\ulnone\i0  embedded assembler.  However, all versions of DIAL can only run on the PDP-12 computer.  Additionally, the DIAL assembler does not support literals.  P?S/8 PAL does not require LINC hardware to assemble LINC mode programs.\par
\par
While the use of literals generally applies to PDP-8 code, certain features can be adapted to LINC mode programming using appropriate addressing restrictions.  Advanced users can contrive viable cases minimally worth pursuing.\par
\par
In general, LINC programming does not require literals due to significantly different addressing modes.  Immediate mode addressing replaces literals because the immediate mode operand cannot be used in other instructions.  Immediate mode specifying the address of common variables can be likened to literals; if the addresses can be contrived to conform to PDP-8 page addressing, actual PDP-8 literals can be used obtaining the intended results.\par
\par
Note: Any form of arithmetic expression used to calculate LINC mode addresses is performed using one's complement arithmetic.\par
\par
All LINC instructions on the PDP-12 and the LINC are implemented in hardware.  On the LINC-8, certain instructions are trapped and emulated using a support program known as \i PROGOFOP\i0  (\i PROG\i0 ram \i OF\i0  \i OP\i0 eration).  PROGOFOP is required for 100% emulation support; however, short sections of LINC programming can be initiated by PDP-8 programming with interrupt-handling optional.\par
\par
Note: The PDP-12 also supports an alternate form of trap simulator to allow programs written for the classic LINC and/or LINC-8 to be run as intended, perhaps with alternate interpretation of the LINCtape class instructions.  For more information, consult DEC documentation of the PDP-12 trap simulator.\page\ul 2.12 LINCtape information\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 and OS/8 run on 128 words/block LINCtapes; \i DIAL\i0  uses extended-length 256 words/block LINCtapes.  Utilities to access 256 words/block LINCtapes require custom programming for each machine.  To transfer assembled programs to the classic LINC requires a custom image utility as conventions for LINC operating systems vary.  LINC systems cannot access PDP-8-oriented LINCtapes; the LINC-8 can handle all formats because the underlying hardware is actually a PDP-8 peripheral.  The PDP-12 includes extended operations to work with all common formats.\par
\par
\ul 2.13 Memory management options\ulnone\par
\par
\tab /C\tab Enable internal reconfiguration of assembler resources. \tab\tab\tab Using the /C option may make certain assembly configurations \tab\tab on smaller machines viable at the expense of performance. \tab\tab\tab See below for additional configuration details.\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is designed to run on 4K or larger machines; additional memory, if available, is used to allow a larger symbol table which is generally the limiting factor with regard to overall program size that can be successfully assembled.\par
\par
When P?S/8 PAL is first loaded, the passed option switches are checked to determine the running configuration size with regard to modular assembler components once the assembly process begins.  Deselected modular sections are removed; retained modular options are then relocated as required to maximize the space available for symbol table and input/output buffers.\par
\par
Depending on options selected, it is possible loading the symbol table can overflow the assembler's capabilities; the selected configuration cannot function with standard allocation of input/output buffers.\par
\par
To achieve a viable configuration, the number of input/output buffers can be reduced; table space is then made available for additional symbols.  The assembly process will take longer due to fewer buffers (but will more likely succeed while handling more symbols).\par
\par
To gauge the magnitude of the problem on 4K systems, the following is a list of the extra memory allocation requirements (over the minimum needed for an assembly without any modular sections retained):\par
\par
\tab a)\tab /N-related \i niceties\i0  options (fully formatted headers on \tab\tab\tab listing particulars) requires one additional page.\par
\par
\tab b)\tab Literal and/or link generation requires three additional \tab\tab\tab pages.\par
\par
\tab c)\tab Enabling dual mode (LINC) assembly requires three additional \tab\tab pages.\par
\par
\tab d)\tab Enabling cross-referenced listing output requires two \tab\tab\tab\tab additional pages.\page Each page retained for symbol table usage adds the ability to support 32 additional symbols; an assembly with no modular options retained can support 288 additional symbols.\par
\par
If the /C option switch is set, the input and output buffers are reduced to a minimum complement causing the assembly process to take considerably more time; however, sufficient memory space becomes available to support any combination of the modular program options as described above.\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL is designed to dynamically take advantage of additional memory if available.  On all systems with 8K, at least another 3K of memory is available for the symbol table; as such, all modular components can generally be retained without restriction.\par
\par
Note: Certain P?S/8 configurations reserve the highest quarter memory field available for the system device handler extension.  For all systems that do not require the handler extension, the full additional 4K of memory can be made available to P?S/8 PAL (and other programs).\par
\par
When 7K (or more) memory is available, symbols are not stored in field 0.  The /C option switch can be used to sacrifice performance while allowing additional symbols residing in field 0.\par
\par
The maximum number of symbols allowed is 4095 including all permanent symbols and directives.  If 16K of memory is available for symbols (20K total memory or more), there is no point in enabling the /C option other than to perform timing tests to measure the resulting performance degradation; the exact configuration with regard to the modular options becomes moot for larger machines.\par
\par
Note: PAL8 requires 24K or more to support the maximum symbol table size of 4095 symbols due to certain design limitations of OS/8.\par
\par
\ul 2.20 P?S/8 PAL assembler performance\ulnone\par
\par
P?S/8 PAL performance was once compared to PAL8 using a deliberately biased configuration and a stopwatch.  While P?S/8 PAL took twice as long to assemble a large file chosen at random from various OS/8 source programs under development at the time, the P?S/8 configuration was based on TC08/TU56 DECtape while the OS/8 configuration was based on the RK8E/RK05 removable cartridge disk (which is inherently a higher performance system).  Given the overhead of the underlying system devices, it is estimated that P?S/8 PAL can assemble large programs as much as four times as fast as OS/8 PAL8 on identical hardware configurations.\par
\par
Note: Performance of the P?S/8 BIN Slurp loader was compared to OS/8 \i ABSLDR\i0  on the same configuration.  The DECtape-based P?S/8 Slurp loader successfully loaded binary files twice as fast as OS/8 ABSLDR on the RK8E/RK05.  This counter-intuitive feat, when demonstrated to various DEC PDP-8 programming group employees, induced a state of cognitive dissonance; they simply could not comprehend the notion that a DECtape could somehow perform faster than an RK05.\page\ul 2.30\tab Additional binary chaining options\ulnone\par
\par
Various options of the P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  utility apply during a successful chain operation.  Assuming no assembly errors, chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  is generally used for the purpose of loading the binary output files created during the assembly.\par
\par
Note: See the description of the /W option switch below for the alternate purpose of chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of punching binary paper-tapes.\par
\par
As explained above, chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  creates a list of binary input files derived from the list of output files created during the assembly; this list may be the direct result of explicitly passed output files or as a result of some particular combined usage of the /B, /D and /U option switches.  As necessary, the list may have been truncated if excess output files were specified.\par
\par
The following additional options of P?S/8 BIN apply if set in the original P?S/8 PAL command line:\par
\par
\tab /G\tab Chain to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of either loading the \tab\tab\tab binary files created during the assembly or punching binary \tab\tab paper-tapes of the binary files (if the /W option switch is \tab\tab also set).  The chain is inhibited if there are assembly \tab\tab\tab errors.\par
\par
\tab /V\tab Enable the virtual binary file loader instead of the \tab\tab\tab\tab hardware-specific binary file loader (if relevant).\par
\par
\tab\tab Where possible, P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  loads binary input files using an \tab\tab implementation of the Slurp loader as originally defined by \tab\tab Richard Lary for the R-L Monitor System.  The P?S/8 \tab\tab\tab\tab implementation\tab  supports loading anywhere within the 32K \tab\tab\tab PDP-8 memory space (although restrictions apply to system\par
 \tab  \tab reserved memory areas).\par
\par
\tab\tab Specifics of the P?S/8 system configuration will indicate \tab\tab\tab whether a hardware-dependent Slurp loader is available; only\par
\tab\tab certain configurations are capable of supporting this.\par
\par
\tab\tab On systems that do not include a hardware-specific Slurp \tab\tab\tab loader, a virtual binary file loader is used that requires \tab\tab\tab the presence of the system device handler.  Other than \tab\tab\tab certain performance issues and minor tradeoffs, the two \tab\tab\tab loaders perform the same overall function.\par
\par
\tab\tab If the /V option switch is set, the virtual binary file \tab\tab\tab loader is used instead of the hardware-specific Slurp\i  \i0 binary \tab\tab file loader.  This is a user preference option assuming the \tab\tab hardware-specific Slurp loader is available; it may be \tab\tab\tab preferable to force the virtual loader under certain \tab\tab\tab\tab circumstances.\page\tab /I\tab If a hardware-dependent Slurp loader is available, the\par
\tab\tab system device handler is reloaded after all binary files are\par
\tab\tab loaded.  As a minor restriction, it is not possible to pass\par
