End of Appendix C\page\f0\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f1\par
Appendix D - P?S/8 PAL command-line option switch ordered summary\par
\par
/A\tab If a symbol table or cross-reference is in effect, all symbols\par
\tab will be used.\par
\par
/B\tab If explicit output files are not specified, the first binary\par
\tab output file is \ul\f0 %\ulnone\f1  on the system device unit.  See the description\par
\tab of the /D and /U option switches.\par
\par
\f0 /C\tab Enable internal reconfiguration of P?S/8 PAL resources to favor\par
\tab symbol table capability over performance.\par
\par
\f1 /D\tab If explicit output files are not specified, the second binary\par
\tab output file is \ul\f0 $\ulnone\f1  on the system device unit.  See the description\par
\tab of the /B and /U option switches.\par
\f0\par
/E\tab Do not retain literal extents when leaving the current page.\par
\par
\tab Note: This option switch is ignored unless some combination of\par
\tab the /Q and /O option switches are in effect.\par
\par
/F\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of punching\par
\tab binary paper-tape output, the output device is the high-speed\par
\tab punch.  See the description of the /R option switch.\par
\par
/G\tab Chain to P?S/8\i  BIN\i0  for the purpose of either loading the binary\par
\tab files created during the assembly or punching binary paper-tapes\par
\tab from the binary files (if the /W option switch is also set).\par
\par
\tab Note: Chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  is inhibited if there were errors\par
\tab detected during the assembly.\par
\par
/H\tab If chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  and the /W option switch is not set, all\par
\tab user memory is preloaded with 7402 (the PDP-8 \i HLT\i0  instruction)\par
\tab prior to binary file loading.  See the description of the /G\par
\tab option switch.\par
\par
/I\tab If chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  and the /W option switch is not set and\par
\tab using a hardware-dependent Slurp loader, the system device \tab handler will be reloaded after all binary files are loaded.\par
\par
\tab Note: This option switch is ignored on systems that only support\par
 \tab the virtual Slurp binary loader.\par
\par
/J\tab Prevent generation of a 12-bit word containing 0000 after text\par
\tab strings with an even count of characters as used with the TEXT\par
\tab and SIXBIT directives.\page\cf2\f1 Appendix D (continued)\par
\par
\cf0\f0 /K\tab Enable the change of title on assembly listing from every input\par
\tab file.  By default, only the first input file supplies the title\par
\tab field for the entire listing.\par
\par
\tab Note: This assumes listing output is enabled.  If there is\par
\tab neither binary file generation nor listing enabled, force a two-\par
 \tab pass assembly.\cf2\f1\par
\par
\cf0\f0 /L\tab Assembly listing output is produced during pass two of the\par
\tab assembly process.  See the description of the /N, /P and /X\par
\tab option switches.\par
\par
/M\tab Chain to P?S/8 \i MAP\i0  to create a bitmap of all binary output\par
\tab generated during the assembly.\par
\par
\tab Note: When chaining to P?S/8 \i MAP\i0  then P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 , the chain to\par
\tab P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  will be inhibited if there were errors detected during\par
\tab the assembly; however, the chain to P?S/8 MAP will always be\par
\tab performed.\par
\par
/N\tab Enable \i neatness\i0  (\i niceties\i0 ) options of an assembly listing.  Page\par
\tab headers are added including the current date, day of the week and\par
\tab page sequence numbers.  See the description of the /T option\par
\tab switch.\par
\par
/O\tab Enable link generation.  Links will be flagged as assembly errors\par
\tab unless the /Q option switch is also set.\par
\par
/P\tab Enable wide-carriage listing output; where applicable, the output\par
\tab will be oriented towards 11" x 17" ledger/tabloid format.  See\par
\tab the description of the /L and /S option switches.\par
\par
/Q\tab Enable generation of Page Zero, current page and conditional\par
\tab literals.  Link generation is disabled.  See the description of\par
\tab the /O option switch.\par
\par
/R\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of punching\par
\tab binary paper-tape output, the tape is punched in enhanced RIM\par
\tab format; this is readable by both the RIM loader and the BIN\par
\tab loader.  See the description of the /F option switch.\par
\par
/S\tab Symbol table output will be created at the end of the assembly.\par
\tab See the description of the /A, /K, /N and /P switches.\par
\par
/T\tab Listing output is forced to the system console.  If the /N option\par
\tab switch is set, tear-off lines will be printed on every page.\par
 \par
\f1 /U\tab If explicit output files are not specified and the /B or /D\par
\tab option switches are in effect, the logical unit used is the\par
\tab system device logical unit number .XOR. 1.\page\cf2 Appendix D (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\f0 /V\tab If chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  and the /W option switch is not set, the\par
\tab virtual Slurp loader will be used to load binary files.\par
\par
\tab Note: Using the /V option switch causes the /I option switch\par
\tab to be moot.  The virtual Slurp loader is used by default on\par
\tab certain system configurations.\cf2\f1\par
\par
\cf0\f0 /W\tab Chain to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  to punch binary paper-tapes from the binary\par
\tab output files.  See the description of the /F and /R switches.\par
\par
\tab Note: Chaining to BIN is inhibited if there were errors detected\par
\tab during the assembly.\cf2\f1\par
\par
\cf0\f0 /X\tab Enable cross-reference listing output of user symbols.  Listing\par
\tab output width is increased to accommodate statement numbers.  See\par
\tab the description of the /A, /N and /P option switches.\cf2\f1\par
\cf0\f0\par
/Y\tab The \highlight3 !\highlight0  operator is changed from \ul\i inclusive OR\ulnone\i0  (.OR) to shift left\par
\tab six bits.\par
\par
/Z\tab If chaining to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  and the /W option switch is not set, all\par
\tab user memory is preloaded with 0000 (the LINC \i HLT\i0  instruction\par
\tab prior to binary file loading.  See the description of the /G\par
\tab option switch.\par
\par
/0\tab Automatically generate *200 after use of the FIELD directive.  By\par
\tab default, only the field change is generated.\par
\par
/1\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 1.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\par
\par
/2\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 2.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\par
\par
/3\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 3.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\par
\par
/4\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 4.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\par
\par
/5\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 5.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\par
\par
/6\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 6.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\page\cf2\f1 Appendix D (continued)\par
\cf0\f0\par
/7\tab If P?S/8 PAL chains to P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  for the purpose of loading the\par
\tab binary output created by the assembly, the program will start in\par
\tab field 7.  See the description of the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter below.\par
\par
/8\tab Enable LINC and PDP-8 dual assembly; the default operating mode\par
\tab is PDP-8 mode (PMODE).  The default assembly address is 0200.\cf2\f1\par
\par
\cf0\f0 /9\tab Enable LINC and PDP-8 dual assembly; the default operating mode \par
\tab is LINC mode (LMODE).  The default assembly address is 4020.\par
\par
=\tab If the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter is explicitly given on the command-line,\par
\tab the program will be started at location xxxx in field 0 (unless\par
\tab one of the extended memory option switches /1 through /7\par
\tab described above are set, in which case the starting field is set\par
\tab to the corresponding field).\par
\par
\tab Note: The program will not be loaded (or started) if there were\par
\tab errors detected during the assembly.\par
